WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.960
Several RAM-backed Linux patterns appear in real systems. The first is live media copied

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.140
into RAM.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.880
A live distribution often starts from a compressed read-only image.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:15.980
With the right distribution support, that image may be copied into memory during boot.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.880
After loading, the system can run with fewer reads from the original boot media.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.460
This is useful for portable environments, recovery sessions,

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:28.000
and machines where the boot device should be removed or used less after startup.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:34.220
A second pattern uses a read-only base image with a writable RAM-backed overlay. The lower

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.680
layer contains the base operating system.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:42.260
The upper layer records changes. A merged view looks writable to the running system. If

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.480
the upper layer is tmpfs,

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.540
changes vanish at reboot.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:51.400
This is common in live systems and can also be useful in kiosks, labs,

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:53.940
and systems that should reset cleanly.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:57.440
A third pattern makes only selected paths volatile.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:03.180
For example, temporary caches, selected logs, browser caches, package download caches,

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:07.640
or scratch directories may live in tmpfs while the rest of the installed system remains

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:08.740
persistent.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.080
This is less dramatic than a full RAM root,

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:17.340
but often easier to support and recover. A fourth pattern is a full volatile root.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:21.000
This is more aggressive. The whole working root may reset every boot,

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:25.620
with persistence only through deliberately mounted paths or save mechanisms.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:30.160
It can suit training machines, clean-room workflows, and controlled appliances,

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.140
but it requires careful planning.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.920
A fifth pattern is hybrid persistence.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:39.780
The base may be read-only, the running changes may be temporary, but /home,

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.200
selected configuration, machine identity, logs, or explicit snapshots may persist.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:50.460
Hybrid systems usually match real operational needs better than pure volatility. The right

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:52.600
pattern depends on the goal.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:55.780
Reducing disk writes, creating a disposable session,

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.440
improving read-heavy boot media behavior, protecting a base image,

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:02.540
and supporting recovery all lead to different designs.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.920
Start with the use case, not with a command copied from another distribution.
